"""Source-specific academic prose, independently read from all seven supplied pages."""
routes=['individual-low','sphere-intermediate','wire-intermediate','wire-high']
def r(sec,key,title,text,page,loc,records=(),scope='source_cohort',kind='reported_source_fact',notes=()):
 return item(sec,key,title,text,page,loc,records,scope,kind,notes=notes)
r('precursors','lead-source','Lead precursor identity','The paper names lead-cyclohexanebutirate (Pb-cHxBu), supplied by STREM or Aldrich at technical grade. The printed name is retained. An exact salt formula, connectivity, hydration state, supplier assignment and precursor preparation are not specified; no definite molecular structure is inferred from the abbreviation.',2,'Experimental section, precursor paragraph',routes)
r('precursors','selenium','Selenium precursor','Selenium is dissolved with the lead precursor in tri-butyl-phosphine (TBP). The abstract and summary describe a TBP/selenium precursor, while the method lists selenium. The source supplies no isolated TBP–Se assay or separate stock concentration; these descriptions do not establish quantitative conversion to one molecular species.',1,'Abstract; p.2 Experimental section',routes);more('selenium',2,'Experimental section, precursor paragraph')
r('precursors','tbp','Tri-butyl-phosphine','TBP is the stock solvent and a component of the stated TOPO/TBP surface coating. Stock compositions are supplied as Se/Pb-cHxBu/TBP mass ratios. The amount injected, absolute stock mass and molarity are not given.',2,'Experimental section, stock and mother-solution paragraphs',routes)
r('precursors','topo','Tri-octyl-phosphine oxide','The mother solution contains 6.0 g TOPO from Aldrich, either 90% or 99% purity. These alternative commercial grades are retained without assigning every figure to a specific grade. The source later discusses possible phosphonic-acid impurities in the 90% material.',2,'Experimental section, mother solution',routes);more('topo',5,'Discussion, TOPO purity and assembly enhancement')
r('precursors','argon','Inert handling','Stocks are prepared at room temperature in a glovebox under standard inert conditions. The heated three-neck flask is operated under flowing argon. Neither pressure nor gas-flow rate is reported.',2,'Experimental section, glovebox and argon flow',routes)
r('precursors','quench-solvents','Quench and purification solvents','A reaction aliquot is injected into 1 mL methanol to stop further growth. Products are centrifuged, then subjected to several redissolution/centrifugation cycles in a methanol–butanol solution. Butanol isomer, solvent ratio, centrifuge speed, duration and exact cycle count are unspecified.',2,'Experimental section, continuation at top of right column',['aliquot-workup'])
r('precursors','low-stock','Low-concentration stock','The method and Results specify Se/Pb-cHxBu/TBP = 0.25:0.6:50 by mass for individual nanocrystals. These are relative mass parts, not three absolute gram charges. The summary instead prints 0.25:0.6:0.5; this conflict is preserved separately.',2,'Experimental section stock (a); Results first paragraph',['individual-low']);more('low-stock',6,'Summary, conflicting low-concentration stock')
r('precursors','intermediate-stock','Intermediate-concentration stock','The method and Results give Se/Pb-cHxBu/TBP = 0.5:1.2:50 by mass. Figure1 and Figure4 captions instead print 0.5:1:50. The reader follows the method/body route description while retaining the conflicting caption values, rather than silently treating them as equivalent.',2,'Experimental section stock (b); Results assembly paragraph',['sphere-intermediate','wire-intermediate']);more('intermediate-stock',3,'Figure1 caption');more('intermediate-stock',4,'Figure4 caption')
r('precursors','high-stock','High-concentration stock','The method gives Se/Pb-cHxBu/TBP = 1:2.5:50 by mass. This composition is explicitly associated with the bent assembly in Figure3 and with the Figure5 device schematic; the body also describes a 40 min high-concentration wire result that is not shown.',2,'Experimental section stock (c)',['wire-high']);more('high-stock',3,'Results, high-concentration wire');more('high-stock',4,'Figure3 caption');more('high-stock',5,'Figure5 caption')
r('precursors','grid-materials','Microscopy support','TEM specimens use a 300-mesh copper grid with amorphous Formvar coating stabilized by evaporated carbon. A small solution droplet is deposited and dried at room temperature. Support materials are not product constituents.',2,'Experimental section, TEM specimen preparation',['microscopy'])
r('precursors','device-materials','Device materials','The electrical device uses a p-doped Si substrate with 200 nm oxide, a hydrophobic treatment named trimethylsilane, PMMA resist, and evaporated Ti/Au contacts. Treatment concentration, reaction conditions, oxide preparation and contact thicknesses are not supplied. The schematic surface groups do not independently resolve the exact treatment reagent.',2,'Experimental section, electrical circuit fabrication',['device-fabrication']);more('device-materials',5,'Figure5A/C, surface and device schematics')

r('protocol','stock-preparation','Stock preparation','Dissolve the source-defined lead and selenium precursors in TBP at room temperature under inert glovebox conditions, using the appropriate printed mass ratio. The paper does not provide dissolution duration or injected stock volume.',2,'Experimental section, stock preparation',routes,'method_context')
r('protocol','mother-solution','Mother-solution preparation','Place 6.0 g TOPO in a three-neck flask and bring the mother solution to 150 °C under flowing argon. No separate evacuation, drying schedule or absolute pressure is stated.',2,'Experimental section, mother solution',routes,'method_context')
r('protocol','injection','Rapid injection and temperature drop','Rapidly inject the precursor stock into the hot mother solution. The reported immediate temperature drop is from 150 to 118 °C. The source supplies no numeric injection rate or duration; the temperature drop is retained as the reported process trajectory.',2,'Experimental section, injection',routes,'method_context')
r('protocol','individual-growth','Individual-nanocrystal branch','For the low-ratio route, retain the reaction at 118 °C. The general method terminates this branch after 15 min by rapidly cooling to 70 °C. Figure1A is a separate approximately 5 min aliquot and must not be labeled as the 15 min endpoint.',2,'Experimental section, branch (a)',['individual-low'],'method_context');more('individual-growth',3,'Figure1A caption')
r('protocol','heated-growth','Assembly branch','The alternative method gradually reheats the solution to 150 °C and extends the reaction at that temperature for up to 150 min. Heating ramp and the exact time origin for every aliquot are not defined. The Discussion also uses the phrase fast heating; no rate is invented to reconcile that wording.',2,'Experimental section, branch (b)',['sphere-intermediate','wire-intermediate','wire-high'],'method_context');more('heated-growth',5,'Discussion, assembly kinetics')
r('protocol','sphere-series','Intermediate-concentration spherical assemblies','The Results associate the intermediate 0.5:1.2:50 stock at 150 °C with spherical assemblies sampled at 10, 25 and 40 min. The abstract/discussion describe the broader spherical-assembly interval as 10–60 min. The caption ratio conflict remains attached to Figure1.',3,'Results and Figure1B–D',['sphere-intermediate'],'method_context')
r('protocol','wire-intermediate','Intermediate-concentration wire branch','The body reports straight wires and approximately 5% junctions after 90 min at 150 °C using the intermediate stock. Figure2 instead labels its wire preparation 1:2.5:50 and its junction as 90 min. These contradictory source descriptions prevent a unique recipe-to-Figure2 batch assignment.',3,'Results wire paragraph; Figure2 caption',['wire-intermediate'],'method_context')
r('protocol','wire-high','High-concentration wire branch','The body reports that increasing the stock mass ratio to 1:2.5:50 produces a wire-like structure after only 40 min, with that particular result not shown. Figure3 and Figure5 use the high-ratio preparation but do not establish that their specific specimens are the 40 min batch.',3,'Results wire paragraph',['wire-high'],'method_context');more('wire-high',4,'Figure3 caption');more('wire-high',5,'Figure5 caption')
r('protocol','aliquot-quench','Aliquot sampling and quench','Withdraw aliquots by syringe every few minutes and inject into 1 mL methanol. The paper identifies this as a growth quench. Aliquot volume and exact sampling schedule are not supplied beyond the named figure time points.',2,'Experimental section, end of left and start of right column',['aliquot-workup'],'method_context')
r('protocol','purification','Purification','Separate the product by centrifugation and perform several redissolution/centrifugation cycles in methanol–butanol solution. The authors report improved purity and monodispersity, but supply no recovery yield, centrifugation settings or quantitative purification comparison.',2,'Experimental section, workup continuation',['aliquot-workup'],'method_context')
r('protocol','microscopy-method','Microscopy and electron diffraction','HR-SEM is described with a Zeiss Leo982 at 4 kV. TEM, energy-dispersive X-ray analysis (EDAX) and selected-area electron diffraction use a JEOL2000FX at 200 kV; high-resolution TEM uses a JEOL3010 at 300 kV. EDAX is named as a method but no elemental spectrum or quantitative composition result is supplied.',2,'Experimental section, instrument paragraph',['microscopy'],'method_context')
r('protocol','microscopy-preparation','TEM specimen preparation','Deposit a small solution droplet onto the Formvar/carbon-coated 300-mesh copper grid and dry at room temperature. This drying temperature belongs to specimen preparation and does not replace the reported synthesis temperatures.',2,'Experimental section, microscopy sample preparation',['microscopy'],'method_context')
r('protocol','absorption-method','Absorption acquisition','Absorption spectra were recorded using a Shimadzu UV–visible spectrometer and a JASCO V-570 UV–visible–near-IR spectrometer. Optical path length, concentration, acquisition temperature and baseline treatment are not stated.',2,'Experimental section, absorption instruments',['absorption'],'method_context')
r('protocol','device-surface','Substrate preparation and alignment','Use p-doped Si with a 200 nm oxide layer, treat the surface with the reagent named trimethylsilane to make it hydrophobic, and define alignment markers by optical lithography. No quantified treatment or lithography recipe is provided.',2,'Experimental section, circuit steps1–2',['device-fabrication'],'method_context')
r('protocol','device-positioning','Wire deposition and selection','Spin-coat the wire dispersion onto the substrate, locate a single wire relative to the markers by SEM, then spin-coat PMMA and anneal. Wire selection is a device-fabrication step; spin speed and annealing conditions are not reported.',2,'Experimental section, circuit steps3–4',['device-fabrication'],'method_context')
r('protocol','device-contacts','Contact patterning and lift-off','Write and develop the contact pattern by electron-beam lithography, evaporate Ti/Au leads, remove the resist and inspect the completed circuit by SEM. Developer, contact thickness and removal solvent are unreported.',2,'Experimental section, circuit steps5–6',['device-fabrication'],'method_context')
r('protocol','contact-optimization','Contact and surface preparation limits','The authors emphasize removing the organic coating at contact points while avoiding unintended surface modification during resist removal. Several annealing temperatures and dissolving solvents were tested until suitable conditions were found, but the selected settings are not disclosed. This is an explicitly incomplete device-processing recipe.',2,'Experimental section, circuit precautions',['device-fabrication'],'method_context')
r('protocol','electrical-method','Electrical acquisition','Current–voltage measurements were performed at room temperature on individual contacted wire-like assemblies. Figure5C includes a Si back gate, but no gate-dependent result is reported in this paper. Higher-field and temperature-dependent measurements are proposed for future mechanism discrimination.',2,'Experimental section, final sentence',['electrical'],'method_context');more('electrical-method',5,'Figure5C/D');more('electrical-method',6,'Discussion and Summary, future electrical measurements')

r('structures','individual-morphology','Individual PbSe nanocrystals','At the low stock ratio and 118 °C, the Results describe a 3.5–10.0 nm size range with distribution below10%, depending on reaction duration. The same paragraph calls the population spherical and then individual particles cubic shaped. These descriptions are preserved rather than converted into a single inferred shape label.',2,'Results, individual nanocrystals',['individual-structure'])
r('structures','individual-hrtem','Individual-particle HRTEM','Figure1A shows an approximately5 nm PbSe crystal after approximately5 min, with a5 nm scale bar. The body assigns rock-salt cubic structure viewed along[100] and a3.05 Å interplanar spacing. This image is a source-defined aliquot, not the complete15 min route endpoint.',2,'Results, single-crystal structure',['individual-structure']);more('individual-hrtem',3,'Figure1A')
r('structures','sphere-morphology','Spherical assembly morphology','Figure1B/C/D shows the10/25/40 min sequence, with200 nm scale bars. The Results give spherical assembly diameters of50–450 nm and comparatively uniform size at each stage; the abstract gives the broader50–500 nm range. These dimensions refer to assemblies, not individual PbSe crystallites.',1,'Abstract',['sphere-structure']);more('sphere-morphology',3,'Figure1B–D and Results')
r('structures','sphere-saed','Polycrystalline assembly diffraction','Figure1E supplies the actual selected-area electron diffraction pattern. The authors interpret it as polycrystalline, with randomly oriented constituent nanocrystals. It is not substituted by a generated pattern and does not establish a refined crystallographic coordinate file.',3,'Figure1E and Results',['sphere-structure'])
r('structures','sphere-growth','Assembly diameter versus time','Figure1F plots assembly diameter in nm against time in min. The body identifies an approximately5 min onset and a plateau above50 min. The original plotted points and line are retained; no exact digitized time–diameter table is manufactured from the graph.',3,'Figure1F and Results',['sphere-structure'])
r('structures','wire-overview','Wire-like assembly size','The abstract describes widths of60–150 nm and lengths of1–5 µm. A separate growth observation describes widths increasing from20 to150 nm in intermediate wires. Neither range is conflated with the widths and contacted lengths of the three electrical specimens.',1,'Abstract',['wire-structure']);more('wire-overview',3,'Results, intermediate wire growth')
r('structures','wire-image','Wire contrast and junction','Figure2A is a bright-field high-resolution image with a40 nm scale bar; darker segments are interpreted as grains near the zone axis. Figure2B shows a junction with a500 nm scale bar. The body reports approximately5% junction formation in the90 min intermediate-ratio case, but the caption’s high-ratio assignment remains unresolved.',3,'Figure2A/B and Results',['wire-structure'])
r('structures','wire-saed','Ordered assembly diffraction','Figure2C shows the original SAED pattern, including the visible020 label. The authors describe a single-crystal-like pattern along[100], an ordered rock-salt cubic structure and lattice constant6.1 Å. Additional microdiffraction is said to support the interpretation but is not shown.',3,'Figure2C and Results',['wire-structure'])
r('structures','wire-orientation','Continuous orientation variation','On slowly rotating the wire, the authors observe dark regions moving along it and describe a helix-like morphology. Adjacent grains differ in orientation by a fraction of a degree, accumulating approximately1–2° over tens of nanometers. The suggested coagulation into a full cylinder is an interpretation, not a direct atomic trajectory.',3,'Results, bright-field contrast and intermediate wires',['wire-structure'])
r('structures','bent-wire','Bent assembly and local fringes','Figure3B shows a bent wire with a10 nm scale bar; A and C magnify its junction and edge. Labels identify3.05 Å(200) fringes and[100] in panelC. The source interprets the coherent fringes as crystalline order built from approximately10 nm cubic nanocrystals, with a6.1 Å rock-salt lattice parameter.',3,'Results, bent wires',['wire-structure']);more('bent-wire',4,'Figure3A–C and caption')
r('structures','device-sem','Contacted-wire image','Figure5B supplies the original SEM image of a wire bridging metal contacts, with a3.00 µm display scale. Its acquisition overlay reads10.0 kV,6.6 mm and×18.0k, whereas the general HR-SEM method states4 kV. These two acquisition contexts remain visible rather than silently harmonized. The representative imaged wire is not assigned to one of the three electrical traces.',5,'Figure5B, SEM annotation',['device-fabrication','electrical']);more('device-sem',2,'Experimental section, HR-SEM voltage')
r('structures','electrical-specimens','Three electrical specimens','The body assigns widths×lengths of110×700 nm to squares,60×1000 nm to circles and150×1200 nm to triangles in Figure5D. Each wire is described as comprising approximately10 nm cubic nanocrystals. These specimen dimensions and symbols remain paired; no single batch identity is inferred for all three.',4,'Results, Figure5 current–voltage specimen dimensions',['electrical']);more('electrical-specimens',5,'Figure5D')
r('structures','structure-boundary','Atomic structure and missing characterization','Rock-salt phase, a6.1 Å lattice parameter and3.05 Å fringes are source observations or assignments. The supplied paper contains no refined atomic coordinates, CIF/VESTA file, XRD pattern, quantitative EDAX spectrum or independently determined ligand coverage. No current-sample photoluminescence or Raman spectrum is supplied. Atomic structure downloads would require a separately labeled reference structure.',2,'Methods and Results; complete supplied source',['source-context'],'curator_interpretation','curator_boundary')

r('properties','absorption-cohorts','Absorption time series','Figure4 shows spherical assembly spectra at30,35 and40 min. The body uses the intermediate0.5:1.2:50 stock, while the caption prints0.5:1:50 at150 °C. Exact identity with the microscopy aliquots is not established solely by matching growth times.',3,'Results, optical paragraph',['absorption']);more('absorption-cohorts',4,'Figure4 caption')
r('properties','absorption-spectrum','Near-IR excitonic absorption','The source describes two prominent excitonic bands between1000 and1600 nm, corresponding to0.775–1.240 eV, blue-shifted from bulk and somewhat broadened relative to isolated nanocrystals. The original graph spans approximately500–2500 nm and includes an upper energy axis. Its ordinate is absorbance in arbitrary units; absolute oscillator strength is not supplied.',3,'Results, optical paragraph',['absorption']);more('absorption-spectrum',4,'Figure4 axes and traces')
r('properties','absorption-shift','Growth-dependent spectral shift','The Discussion describes a small approximately0.04 eV red shift with additional heating and an approximately0.5 eV blue shift relative to the bulk exciton. These are separate comparisons. The persistence of resolved size-dependent bands is interpreted as evidence that constituent nanocrystals remain optically distinct.',4,'Discussion, optical shifts',['absorption']);more('absorption-shift',5,'Discussion continuation')
r('properties','iv-curves','Current–voltage response','Figure5D presents three room-temperature current–voltage curves, with current in nA and voltage in mV. The plotted relationship is approximately linear. The authors take this as evidence against a contact Schottky barrier under these measurement conditions; it is not a universal contact characterization.',4,'Results, electrical measurements',['electrical']);more('iv-curves',5,'Figure5D')
r('properties','conductivity','Conductivity and resistivity','From slopes normalized by wire dimensions, the paper reports resistivity0.15 Ω cm and conductivity approximately7.0 Ω⁻¹ cm⁻¹. The body states an applied field3.5×10⁴ V m⁻¹. Individual fitted slopes, uncertainties and separate conductivity values for each trace are not tabulated.',4,'Results, electrical measurements and continuation',['electrical'])
r('properties','electrical-scope','Electrical scope and future measurements','The paper calls these preliminary room-temperature measurements. Neither variable-temperature transport nor a gate-response curve is supplied. The authors state that the conduction mechanism is not yet conclusive and propose temperature, higher-field and gated measurements.',6,'Discussion opening and Summary',['electrical','source-context'])
r('properties','literature-optical','Cited PbSe optical and thermoelectric context','The introduction cites PbSe band-edge transitions tunable over0.5–1.1 eV, small Stokes shifts and sub-microsecond lifetime; it also cites short-lived1S↔1P/1P↔1D intraband processes and increased Seebeck coefficient. These are external literature context, not measurements of the assemblies in this paper.',1,'Introduction, final paragraph',['source-context'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context');more('literature-optical',2,'Introduction continuation, references50–53')
r('properties','conductivity-comparators','Cited transport comparators','The source compares approximately7.0 Ω⁻¹ cm⁻¹ with55×10⁻³ Ω⁻¹ cm⁻¹ for polycrystalline PbSe films containing micrometer grains and approximately10–20 Ω⁻¹ cm⁻¹ for a PbSe two-dimensional quantum well. These reference66/67 values are not additional specimens or recipes in this review.',6,'Discussion, comparison(e)',['transport-model'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')

r('intuition','motivation','Assembly design rationale','The paper frames nanocrystals as building blocks for channels with controlled spacing and morphology. It distinguishes polycrystalline spherical assemblies with random particle orientation from anisotropic wires with extended crystalline coherence, aiming to connect organization to optical and electrical behavior.',1,'Introduction, assembly motivation',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation');more('motivation',2,'Introduction, scope of present study');more('motivation',4,'Discussion, morphology and coherence')
r('intuition','prior-nanocrystals','Colloidal nanocrystal background','The introduction cites prior spherical nanocrystals with size distributions below5%, organic ligands or epitaxial inorganic shells, and quantum-size/surface-dependent properties. Facet stabilization, coordinating templates, electrochemistry and vapor–liquid–solid methods are cited shape-control approaches. LEDs, photovoltaics, circuitry and biological markers motivate the field; these background results are not measured outputs of this PbSe study.',1,'Introduction, nanocrystal synthesis, shape and device context',['source-context'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')
r('intuition','assembly-literature','Prior assembly and collective-property studies','The source cites1D/2D/3D nanocrystal organization, reversible metal–Mott-insulator behavior of compressed Ag monolayers and predicted nonlinear-optical enhancement as interparticle distance decreases. Metal and magnetic assemblies and CdS/CdSe/InP/PbSe semiconductor superlattices provide comparisons. Name mentions are not additional synthesis records or measured material contributions.',1,'Introduction, prior assembly and collective properties',['source-context'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')
r('intuition','intro-dimensions','Overview dimensions and source scope','The introduction summarizes approximately100 nm polycrystalline spheres and approximately60 nm×1 µm ordered wires formed through concentration changes and heating between118 and150 °C. These overview dimensions are distinct from later population ranges and exact electrical-device dimensions.',2,'Introduction, paragraph describing the present study',['source-context'],'source_cohort','reported_source_fact')
r('intuition','bulk-context','Why PbSe','The introduction gives bulk PbSe a rock-salt structure, direct0.28 eV band gap at300 K at L, high-frequency dielectric constant24.0, and electron/hole effective masses below0.1 m*. It quotes a46 nm effective exciton Bohr radius, about eight times CdSe. These cited bulk properties motivate strong quantum confinement; they are not measured assembly properties.',1,'Introduction, bulk PbSe properties',['source-context'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')
r('intuition','prior-synthesis','Prior synthesis context','The source cites individual PbSe nanocrystals,2.5–7 nm PbSe/PbS core/shell particles with improved photoluminescence, and alkyl-diamine-directed individual wires20 nm×1 µm, rods20×100 nm and100 nm cubes. These external examples motivate the present TOPO/TBP approach and do not supply complete recipes here.',2,'Introduction, references54–56',['source-context'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')
r('intuition','assembly-controls','Parameters affecting assembly','The authors identify nanocrystal size/shape uniformity, ligand coating, interparticle spacing, temperature and concentration as assembly variables. Increased concentration and additional heating support aggregation, while longer time is proposed to permit more ordered arrangements. These qualitative relationships are not a fitted optimization model.',4,'Discussion opening',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation');more('assembly-controls',5,'Discussion, kinetics')
r('intuition','spacing','Interparticle separation','The Discussion describes possible separations from intimate contact to approximately5 nm depending on organic coating, and estimates approximately1.1 nm for TOPO/TBP-coated particles here. Ligand interdigitation or reduced coverage could shorten the separation. No direct separation-distribution measurement is supplied.',4,'Discussion, inter-NC interactions',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_estimate')
r('intuition','coupling-boundary','Exchange and dipolar interaction regimes','Cited work is used to distinguish separations below0.5 nm, where electronic wavefunction overlap/exchange may arise, from larger separations dominated by dipolar coupling. The text distinguishes permanent charge-displacement dipoles from optical transition dipoles. This is a literature-guided rationale, not a measured sharp threshold for the present sample.',4,'Discussion, reference58–60 context',['assembly-model'],'cited_context','cited_literature_context')
r('intuition','ligand-heating','Heating and ligand interpretation','The authors suggest that heating partly removes TOPO, reducing spacing toward0.6 nm and changing the effective dielectric environment; particle growth and added coupling could both contribute to the red shift. The spacing and removal interpretation rely partly on cited work and are not direct ligand-assay results.',4,'Discussion, red shift and ligand removal',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation');more('ligand-heating',5,'Discussion continuation, references45/61')
r('intuition','permanent-dipoles','Proposed permanent dipole sources','Small deviations at cube corners, carriers localized at surface defects or strain, and mutual polarization of neighboring particles are proposed origins of permanent dipoles. The paper favors permanent dipole coupling while the constituent quantum-size features remain visible. No direct dipole-moment measurement is reported.',5,'Discussion, permanent dipoles',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation')
r('intuition','dipole-equation','Dipole-moment model','The source prints μ=6erε₂∞/(ε₂∞+2ε₁∞), defines ε₂∞=24.0 for PbSe and ε₁∞=2.1 for TOPO, and calls r the individual nanocrystal diameter. It estimates μ=500 D for a10 nm particle. These are author model values; the radius/diameter convention is not silently repaired or used for a new calculation.',5,'Discussion, inline dipole equation',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','interaction-energy','Dipole-interaction energy estimate','The printed energy expression uses a single μ, not μ²: ΔH=−μ/2πε₀r(r²−D²), with D defined as surface-to-surface separation and ε₀=8.9×10⁻¹² C² J⁻¹ m⁻¹. The authors report −28 kJ mol⁻¹ for10 nm PbSe particles. Original typography is retained because the expression and conventions need independent dimensional clarification.',5,'Discussion, inline dipole–dipole energy',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','entropy','Free-energy interpretation and limitation','The authors state that assembly has negative entropy change and infer spontaneous ΔG=ΔH−TΔS<0. Negative ΔS alone does not establish that sign: the enthalpy and temperature terms must also be evaluated. The original claim is preserved as author interpretation, with this logical limitation made explicit.',5,'Discussion, column transition and free-energy expression',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation')
r('intuition','kinetic-ordering','Assembly kinetics and alignment hypothesis','The Discussion attributes10–60 min spherical assembly formation to mutual diffusion and proposes that more than60 min or higher concentration allows ordered configurations. Polarization-axis alignment is offered as the reason for wire growth. This mechanism remains a hypothesis distinct from the observed images.',5,'Discussion, assembly timing',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation')
r('intuition','topo-impurities','TOPO purity and wire formation','The authors suggest that phosphonic-acid impurities in90% TOPO selectively stabilize facets, favor elongated constituents and increase wire assembly formation by approximately15%. The impurity identity/concentration, comparison basis and sample-by-sample grade assignment are not reported.',5,'Discussion, TOPO90% and reference12',['assembly-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation')
r('intuition','transport-options','Possible transport mechanisms','The paper considers resonant tunneling between discrete levels, thermal activation to a mobility edge and variable-range hopping. It describes dipolar coupling as a proposed connection between neighboring particles while organic ligands can impose a transport barrier. These alternatives were not distinguished experimentally in the reported room-temperature dataset.',5,'Discussion, electrical mechanisms',['transport-model'],'model_context','author_interpretation');more('transport-options',6,'Discussion opening')
r('intuition','internal-field','Internal-field and dielectric model','The source estimates internal field using E≈V/(εₘL), where L is wire length and εₘ is a printed volume-weighted dielectric expression involving ε₁∞,ε₂∞,r and D. Its unusual mathematical typography is preserved in the original crop; no corrected effective-medium equation is substituted. With r=10 nm,D=1.1 nm and V=35 mV, it reports E=7.0×10³ V m⁻¹ and resistance about18 kΩ over a coated nanocrystal.',6,'Discussion, calculation(a)',['transport-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','capacitance','Capacitance and RC estimate','Using the printed C=4πε₀εₘ(r+D), the source calculates self-capacitance1.5×10⁻¹⁸ F and RC time2.7×10⁻¹⁴ s. These quantities belong to its isolated-particle model; they are not direct capacitance or lifetime measurements.',6,'Discussion, calculation(b)',['transport-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','transfer-energy','Transfer energy and level discreteness','The source sets τ=RC and estimates E_T=πℏ/τ=76 meV, comparing it with approximately100 meV conduction intraband spacing. This comparison is used to suggest that discrete levels influence conduction and may support resonant tunneling. It does not conclusively identify a transport mechanism.',6,'Discussion, calculation(c) and reference65',['transport-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','barrier','Interparticle potential and cited barrier','The authors estimate eED=7.0 µeV across the interparticle gap and compare it with a cited20 µeV TOPO-related barrier. The printed microelectronvolt units are retained; the latter value is external reference61 context. Neither is a directly measured barrier distribution in these wires.',6,'Discussion, calculation(d) and reference61',['transport-model'],'model_context','author_model')
r('intuition','outlook','Source outlook','The source proposes further temperature-dependent electrical measurements, higher fields and gate-dependent studies to establish the conduction mechanism. No such follow-up results are included in the supplied article, and the external references have not been re-read as part of this source review.',6,'Discussion and Summary',['source-context'],'model_context','author_outlook')

r('sources','identity','Source identity','A. Sashchiuk, L. Amirav, M. Bashouti, M. Krueger, U. Sivan and E. Lifshitz, PbSe Nanocrystal Assemblies: Synthesis and Structural, Optical, and Electrical Characterization. Nano Letters2004,4(1),159–165; DOI10.1021/nl0345116. ReceivedJuly14,2003; revisedNovember10,2003; published onlineNovember26,2003. The issue year2004 is retained despite the2003 online date.',1,'Title, author block, dates and DOI',['source-context'],'source_metadata','bibliographic_metadata')
r('sources','coverage','Supplied-document coverage','All seven supplied main-article pages were read and visually inspected. The incoming and legacy copies are byte-identical. The article contains five numbered figures, no tables and several unnumbered inline model expressions. No supporting-information declaration or matched local SI was found in the present review bundle.',1,'Complete main article and local source identity',['source-context'],'source_metadata','coverage_metadata')
r('sources','administrative','Administrative metadata','The ACS copyright and price line, correspondence address and manuscript code NL0345116 are administrative metadata. The2026 download watermark is not an experiment or publication date. The PDF metadata title No Job Name and malformed creation date are not substituted for the verified printed title and dates.',1,'Publisher footer, download watermark and PDF metadata',['source-context'],'source_metadata','bibliographic_metadata')
r('sources','ratio-conflicts','Precursor-ratio conflicts','Methods/Results intermediate ratio0.5:1.2:50 conflicts with Figure1/4 captions0.5:1:50. Figure2 caption uses1:2.5:50 while the accompanying body associates the90 min wire/junction example with intermediate ratio. Summary low-ratio0.25:0.6:0.5 conflicts with the method/body value0.25:0.6:50. All are retained with location; no corrected recipe is fabricated.',2,'Experimental section; p.3 Results/Figure1–2; p.4 Figure4; p.6 Summary',['source-context'],'curator_interpretation','source_conflict');more('ratio-conflicts',3,'Figure1 and Figure2 captions');more('ratio-conflicts',4,'Figure4 caption');more('ratio-conflicts',6,'Summary stock ratio')
